A||||2||RacinRob||2M4 to 3.4 TDC||||1||
Z||000000||RacinRob||12-12-2001||11:23 AM||||I know there has been talk on here about switching a 4 cyc to a 6cyc 2.8, but what about switching a 4cyc to a 3.4 TDC. the reason for this is that I what an 86 or new se, without power options (I really want to save on weight.) However the few 6 cyc I have found have have all the power options or they are in terrible shape, so now I am looking at a 4 to 6 swap?<P>thanks for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||DKOV||12-12-2001||12:04 PM||||The Fiero is a Fiero when it comes to motor swaps.  You're putting something in there that it was not designed for.  As such, there will ALWAYS be a way with the additioan of some minor parts and some creativity.<P>The only drawback that I can think of with your proposed 2m4 swap is the REST of the car...  In order to keep the car straight and firmly planted, you are going to need to improve the suspension and steering components to 88 specs at least!<P>Depending on the 3.4 you choose, pushrod or DOHC, you will find the car VERY hard to control when pushed.  And don't tell me you won't push it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Besides, the extra weight that electric windows adds is minimal.  You can shave that by getting a lightweight battery!<P>Good Luck with your search!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||12-12-2001||08:45 PM||||What tranny are you planning on using?<BR>A Fiero Getrag would sure make it easier,then you'd still need to change the fuel pump,tach,in addition to the rest of the swap stuff.<BR>DKOV is right about tightening up the suspension;horsepower changes things,and the car really can be super easy to drive when setup well.There are several companies like Ground Control that have recently been selling coil over kits for bargain prices,this gives you a selection of springs to choose from.Somewhere in the neighborhood of 300# springs,with a 25# difference between the front(-) and rear(+) should work pretty good.Then you'll be needing some wider wheels and tires,go with about an inch wider in the rear and you'll be all set,but then there's the brakes...<BR>||||reg||1
